```c
/* Example of logic, not a complete program: lock() waits until the resource is free. */
void job_one(void)
{
    lock(&printer);
    lock(&scanner);
    /* ... use both ... */
    unlock(&scanner);
    unlock(&printer);
}

void job_two(void)
{
    lock(&scanner);     /* opposite order: circular wait is possible */
    lock(&printer);
    unlock(&printer);
    unlock(&scanner);
}
```
If `job_one` holds the printer and `job_two` holds the scanner, each waits for the other for ever. The cure is to make `job_two` lock the printer first, so that all jobs request resources in the same order and no cycle can form. This code is an example of logic; it was not compiled. Its behaviour was checked by a Python program that tried every possible interleaving of the two jobs: the opposite orders can deadlock and the common order never does.
